<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Model Details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix, we provide additional definitions and details on <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CRaWl</span>, including the random walks that we sample and the exact architecture of the CNN in each layer.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Random Walks</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A walk of length <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> in a graph <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of nodes <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{0},\dots,v_{\ell})\in V^{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{0},\dots,v_{\ell})\in V^{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i-1}v_{i}\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i-1}v_{i}\in E</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[\ell]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[\ell]</annotation></semantics></math>.
A random walk in a graph is obtained by starting at some initial node <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and then iteratively sampling the next node <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> randomly from the neighbors <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> of the current node <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider two different random walk strategies: <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform</em> and <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-backtracking</em>.
The uniform walks are obtained by sampling the next node uniformly from all neighbors:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i+1}\sim\mathcal{U}\big(N_{G}(v_{i})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i+1}\sim\mathcal{U}\big(N_{G}(v_{i})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">On sparse graphs with nodes of small degree (such as molecules) this walk strategy has a tendency to backtrack often.
This slows the traversal of the graph and interferes with the discovery of long-range patterns.
The non-backtracking walk strategy addresses this issue by excluding the previous node from the sampling (unless the degree is one):</p>
<table id="A1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{i+1}\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{NB}}(v_{i})\quad\text{with}\quad\mathcal{D}_{\text{NB}}(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>NB</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>with</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>NB</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{i+1}\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{NB}}(v_{i})\quad\text{with}\quad\mathcal{D}_{\text{NB}}(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!=\!\begin{cases}\mathcal{U}\big(N_{G}(v_{i})\big),\!\!\!&amp;\text{if $i\!=\!0\lor\text{deg}(v_{i})\!=\!1$}\\
\mathcal{U}\big(N_{G}(v_{i})\!\setminus\!\{v_{i-1}\}\big)\!,\!\!&amp;\text{else}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mtext>deg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>else</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!=\!\begin{cases}\mathcal{U}\big(N_{G}(v_{i})\big),\!\!\!&amp;\text{if $i\!=\!0\lor\text{deg}(v_{i})\!=\!1$}\\
\mathcal{U}\big(N_{G}(v_{i})\!\setminus\!\{v_{i-1}\}\big)\!,\!\!&amp;\text{else}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The choice of the walk strategy is a hyperparameter of <span id="A1.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CRaWl</span>.
In our experiments the non-backtracking strategy usually performs better as shown in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Ablation Study ‣ Walking Out of the Weisfeiler Leman Hierarchy: Graph Learning Beyond Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Convolution Module</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we describe the architecture used for the 1D CNN network <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CNN}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>CNN</mtext><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CNN}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in each layer <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Conv1D}(d,d^{\prime},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Conv1D</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Conv1D}(d,d^{\prime},k)</annotation></semantics></math> be a standard 1D convolution with input feature dimension <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, output feature dimension <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, kernel size <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and no bias.
This module has <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\cdot d^{\prime}\cdot k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\cdot d^{\prime}\cdot k</annotation></semantics></math> trainable parameters.
The term scales poorly for larger hidden dimensions <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, since the square of this dimension is scaled with an additional factor of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, which we typically set to 9 or more.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue we leverage <em id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Depthwise Separable Convolutions</em>, as suggested by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chollet (2017)</a></cite>.
This method is most commonly applied to 2D data in Computer Vision, but it can also be utilized for 1D convolutions.
It decomposes one convolution with kernel size <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> into two convolutions:
The first convolution is a standard 1D convolution with kernel size 1.
The second convolution is a depthwise convolution with kernel size <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, which convolves each channel individually and therefore only requires <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\cdot d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\cdot d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters.
The second convolution is succeeded by a Batch Norm layer and a ReLU activation function.
Note that there is no non-linearity between the two convolutions.
These operations effectively simulate a standard convolution with kernel size <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> but require substantially less memory and runtime.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After the ReLU activation, we apply an additional (standard) convolution with kernel size <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by another ReLU non-linearity.
This final convolution increases the expressiveness of our convolution module which could otherwise only learn linearly separable functions.
This would limit its ability to distinguish the binary patterns that encode identity and adjacency.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The full stack of operations effectively applies a 2-layer MLP to each sliding window position of the walk feature tensor.
Overall, <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CNN}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>CNN</mtext><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CNN}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of the following operations:
<span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:469.8pt;">
<span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:610.7pt;">
<span id="A1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<span><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Conv1D}(d,d^{\prime},1)\rightarrow\text{Conv1D}^{dw}(d^{\prime},d^{\prime},k)\rightarrow\text{BatchNorm}\rightarrow\text{ReLU}\rightarrow\text{Conv1D}(d^{\prime},d^{\prime},1)\rightarrow\text{ReLU}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Conv1D</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>Conv1D</mtext><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>BatchNorm</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext>Conv1D</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>ReLU</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Conv1D}(d,d^{\prime},1)\rightarrow\text{Conv1D}^{dw}(d^{\prime},d^{\prime},k)\rightarrow\text{BatchNorm}\rightarrow\text{ReLU}\rightarrow\text{Conv1D}(d^{\prime},d^{\prime},1)\rightarrow\text{ReLU}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span>
Here, <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Conv1D</span> is a standard 1D convolution and <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Conv1D}^{dw}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Conv1D</mtext><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Conv1D}^{dw}</annotation></semantics></math> is a depthwise convolution.
The total number of parameters of one such module (without the affine transformation of the Batch Norm) is equal to <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dd^{\prime}+kd+{d^{\prime}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>d</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dd^{\prime}+kd+{d^{\prime}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Architecture</h3>

